I can’t put my name on a safety assertion or act as the accountable approver. That sign-off needs to come from someone on your team who can verify the build configuration, distribution path, and access controls.

I can help with a narrowly scoped assertion they could review and sign:

> **Demo-build risk acceptance:** The designated build owner confirms that client-reported hit positions are enabled only in the access-controlled demo configuration; the feature is disabled by default and unavailable in staging, public-test, and release builds; demo clients cannot connect to production services; and CI includes a release-build check that fails if the demo flag is enabled. This exception expires after the publisher demo and does not constitute approval for production use.

Before anyone signs, I’d require:

- a compile-time exclusion—not merely a runtime toggle;
- separate demo credentials and servers;
- production connection endpoints removed or blocked;
- an automated CI assertion for shipping builds;
- telemetry clearly identifying unvalidated hits;
- a named owner and dated removal ticket;
- one independent person verifying the packaged demo binary.

That gives the signer something concrete and testable to attest to, rather than a vague claim that the shortcut is “safe.”
